---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import ConfigMapGenerator from '../../components/ConfigMapGenerator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Kubernetes ConfigMap Generator" description="Generate a ConfigMap YAML from a simple list of key-value pairs.">
  <ConfigMapGenerator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>What ConfigMaps are for</h2>
  <p>ConfigMaps let you separate configuration from container images — the same image can run in dev, staging, and production with different config injected via ConfigMap, without rebuilding the image for each environment.</p>

  <h2>How to use this generator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Enter your key-value configuration pairs.</li>
    <li>Copy the generated ConfigMap YAML.</li>
    <li>Apply it with <code>kubectl apply -f configmap.yaml</code>, then reference it from your Deployment.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>A ConfigMap with <code>LOG_LEVEL: info</code> can be mounted as an environment variable in a pod spec via <code>envFrom.configMapRef</code>, making the value available to the container at runtime.</p>

  <h2>Common ConfigMap Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Putting secrets in a ConfigMap.</strong> ConfigMaps are stored as plain text — use a Secret resource for passwords, API keys, or tokens instead.</li>
    <li><strong>Forgetting pods don't automatically pick up ConfigMap changes</strong> — depending on how it's mounted, a pod restart is often required to see updated values.</li>
    <li><strong>Not specifying a namespace</strong> when your Deployment expects the ConfigMap in a specific namespace other than default.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This ConfigMap Generator</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Injecting environment-specific configuration into a Deployment</li>
    <li>Sharing non-sensitive configuration across multiple pods</li>
    <li>Mounting a configuration file into a container as a volume</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Kubernetes ConfigMap FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>How is this different from a Secret?</strong> Secrets are intended for sensitive data and are base64-encoded (not encrypted by default) with additional access controls — ConfigMaps are for non-sensitive configuration only.</p>
</ToolLayout>
